HOW OUTPUT IS ATTRIBUTED
------------------------
Every artefact this tool generates carries its origin, in the channel that
artefact's own format provides — never painted onto the content:

  PNG screenshots   tEXt chunks: Software, Author, Copyright, Comment,
                    qaw-provenance, qaw-session. No pixel is altered.
  Markdown          an HTML comment carrying the token
  JSON, Postman     a `_provenance` object
  YAML, CSV         a leading comment line
  JUnit / XML       an XML comment
  HTML              meta tags, a schema.org JSON-LD block, and a corner mark
  Generated code    a two-line comment banner
  Jira and trackers a one-line colophon plus an invisible issue property

Each carries a token of the form:

  qaw1:<base64url payload>.<fnv1a checksum>

The payload names the product, the owner, the version, the artefact kind, the
session and the time. It is verifiable with the checksum, so a payload that has
been edited can be detected.

THIS IS ATTRIBUTION, NOT A SEAL
-------------------------------
The checksum makes tampering detectable, not impossible. A browser extension
cannot hold a private key that the person running it cannot also read, so no
claim of cryptographic provenance is made or implied. Anyone determined can
strip any of these marks. What they do is make accidental loss of attribution
very unlikely, and deliberate removal demonstrable.

